  (W, "Proof of work is a piece of data that takes a great deal of computation to find and almost none to check. The chapter builds the idea from the hash function: a miner feeds a candidate block into an algorithm that scrambles the data, producing output that looks nothing like the input; the same input always gives the same output, but nobody can predict the output for a new input; and if the output matches a template determined by the protocol, the block is accepted [AH]. The chapter then names the result: the simple verification process probabilistically proves the work was done, so the data necessary to generate that proof, in this case the block, is called proof of work [AH]."),
  (Y, "Proof of work is what makes the record expensive to rewrite, and therefore what makes a confirmed payment reliable. The chapter puts the number on it: as of writing, the number of candidate blocks miners need to try before finding a winning combination is about 168 billion trillion, and that is also how many times the hash function needs to be run; yet once a winning combination has been found, anyone can verify the block is valid by running the hash function just once [AH]. Changing a recorded transaction would mean redoing that work and then outpacing everyone else, which is the argument the security group develops."),
  (E, "The reader meets proof of work as the five minutes between Alice's payment and its first confirmation, and as the electricity bill of the mining industry. The book's mining chapter gives a miniature version that can be run by hand: hash the phrase Hello, world! followed by a number and look for a result that begins with a zero. It finds the answer at the number 32 and remarks that although it only takes one hash computation to verify, it took thirty-two to find [AH]. The owner's course notes describe the same mechanism as repeatedly hashing the header of the block with a random number until a solution matching a predetermined pattern emerges [AL]."),
  (H, "The search is a loop with one changing field. The example above is the demonstration version used by the interactive page of the practice branch: hashing a block number, a nonce and the text of a payment, and looking for a digest that begins with four zeros, the first nonce that succeeds is 6630. Four leading hexadecimal zeros mean that about one attempt in sixteen to the fourth power, that is one in 65,536, should work, so a few thousand attempts is the expected order. Bitcoin's real condition is the same test with a far smaller threshold: the double hash of the eighty-byte header, read as a number, must be below the target that the difficulty concept explains."),
